

Feature: make the dashboard's wall-clock refresh interval admin-configurable instead of hardcoded, and make the load-failed indicator consistent across all widgets.

Context: widget_scheduler.py currently registers a single shared APScheduler job with CronTrigger(minute="0,15,30,45"), so every enabled widget times every enabled application refreshes every 15 minutes. run_startup_catchup() separately refreshes any cache entry in widget_cache_store that is missing or older than a hardcoded 15 minutes. widget_cache.py already persists each widget's last successfully fetched data keyed by (widget_id, application_id), with updated_at and trigger_source, using last-good-data semantics where a failed run leaves the existing row untouched.

Requirements:

1. Add a single global setting, refresh_interval_minutes, that replaces the hardcoded 15-minute value everywhere it is currently used (the wall-clock cron in start_widget_scheduler and the staleness check in run_startup_catchup). Persist this setting so it survives restarts. Default it to 30 minutes if nothing has been configured yet.

2. Expose this setting in the Widget Control section of the admin console as a dropdown, not free text, since a bad free-text value could break the schedule. Offer these options in minutes: 15, 30, 45, 60, 90, 120, 180, 360. Leave room to add larger options later (for example 720 or 1440) without significant rework.

3. When the admin changes this value, reschedule the existing scheduler job immediately, with no restart required, so the new interval takes effect on the next tick.

4. All eight interval options divide evenly into 24 hours, so implement the schedule as ticks spaced evenly from local midnight (Asia/Kolkata) rather than counting from whenever the setting happens to change. In practice this likely means recalculating the trigger's start point to the most recent local midnight whenever the interval changes, then repeating every refresh_interval_minutes from there, instead of trying to express every option as a fixed CronTrigger minute list. It's fine if the resulting tick times feel slightly arbitrary for some options (45 minutes gives 00:00, 00:45, 01:30, 02:15, and so on) as long as the exact tick time actually used is always visible next to the data it produced.

5. In run_startup_catchup, replace the hardcoded 15-minute check with the currently configured refresh_interval_minutes. On startup, for every (widget_id, application_id) entry, compare its stored updated_at against now. Only refresh entries older than the configured interval; leave fresher entries alone and serve their existing cached data. After a long outage (app down for hours while the interval is set to, say, one hour), each stale entry should refresh exactly once at startup, not once per missed interval.

6. In widget_cache.py, alongside the existing data and updated_at fields, persist a status field per (widget_id, application_id) recording whether the most recent run succeeded or failed. Keep the last-good-data behavior (a failed run doesn't overwrite prior good data), but status should always reflect the latest run's outcome, so a failure is visible right after a restart without needing a new run to discover it.

7. The manual refresh button's trigger behavior is unchanged, but its result must update the same data, updated_at, and status fields above, since both the wall-clock cron and startup catch-up key off those fields.

8. Surface the exact last-successful-refresh timestamp for each widget (and per application within it) in the dashboard UI itself, not just an admin/debug view.

9. Fix the load-failed indicator so it behaves the same on every widget. Pod Count Summary correctly shows a warning-colored load-failed state per application on failure; Resource Utilization doesn't, for the same kind of failure. Since every widget runs on the same shared job-execution and caching pipeline across all onboarded applications, move this logic out of individual widget components and into the same shared layer that owns the status field from point 6, so any widget, current or future, automatically shows load-failed for any application whose latest run didn't succeed, while succeeding applications display normally. Audit Resource Utilization specifically to confirm it reads from this shared status field rather than only checking whether cached data exists.

Confirm the plan for points 4 and 9 before implementing, since point 4 changes how the scheduler is built and point 9 touches shared rendering logic used by multiple widgets.
